    "text": "All the evidence is in place. Here is the analysis, grounded in this checkout.\n\n## Setup: what `zvec.init(optimize_threads=4)` establishes\n\n`python/zvec/zvec.py:162-163` puts `optimize_threads` into the config dict; `src/binding/python/model/common/python_config.cc:154-158` validates it (must be positive) and sets `ConfigData::optimize_thread_count = 4`. `GlobalConfig::initialize` (`src/db/common/config.cc:119-141`) is one-shot (atomic CAS, lines 121-124) and ends by calling `GlobalResource::Instance().initialize()` (line 140), which under `std::call_once` creates the global optimize pool as `new ailego::ThreadPool(optimize_thread_count(), optimize_thread_binding())` (`src/db/common/global_resource.cc:21-29`; accessor at `src/db/common/global_resource.h:31-34`). The `ThreadPool` constructor clamps size to `[1, hardware_concurrency]` (`src/ailego/parallel/thread_pool.cc:85-101`), so with hardware_concurrency \u2265 4 the global optimize pool has exactly **4 workers**; binding defaults to false (`src/db/common/config.cc:43`).\n\n## 1. Pool selection for A vs. B\n\nBoth invocations flow: `python_collection.cc:240` (`Optimize` binding) \u2192 `CollectionImpl::optimize` (`src/db/collection.cc:952`), which passes `options.concurrency_` into `build_compact_task` (`src/db/collection.cc:988-990`; `OptimizeOptions{concurrency_}` defined at `src/include/zvec/db/options.h:60-62`) \u2192 the compact task calls `SegmentHelper::ReduceVectorIndex(..., task.concurrency_, ...)` (`src/db/index/segment/segment_helper.cc:148-150`) \u2192 `MergeWithOptionalReuse` (`segment_helper.cc:668-671`, also 695/743 for quantized fields).\n\nThe selection happens in two places:\n\n- `SegmentHelper::MergeWithOptionalReuse` (`src/db/index/segment/segment_helper.cc:799-811`): if `concurrency == 0`, `merge_options.pool = GlobalResource::Instance().optimize_thread_pool()` and `write_concurrency = pool->count()` (lines 805-808); otherwise `pool` stays `nullptr` and `write_concurrency = concurrency` (lines 809-811).\n- `Index::merge` (`src/core/interface/index.cc:1416-1446`), reached via `VectorColumnIndexer::merge` (`src/db/index/column/vector_column/vector_column_indexer.cc:103-134`, forwarding `{write_concurrency, pool}` at 129-131): if `options.pool != nullptr`, the reducer **borrows** it via `set_thread_pool` (`src/include/zvec/core/framework/index_reducer.h:173-175,183`) and `effective_write_concurrency = min(write_concurrency, pool->count())` (lines 1436-1439). If `pool == nullptr`, it creates a function-local `ailego::ThreadPool(options.write_concurrency, false)` whose lifetime explicitly covers `reduce()` (comment line 1433; lines 1440-1445), and the reducer borrows that.\n\nSo:\n\n- **A (`concurrency=0`)**: the **global optimize pool with 4 workers** is selected and borrowed; `write_concurrency = effective = 4`.\n- **B (`concurrency=2`)**: a **temporary, merge-local pool with 2 workers** (clamp leaves 2 unchanged since hw \u2265 4) is created inside `Index::merge` and destroyed when the merge returns.\n- **B does not change the global pool.** The global pool is created once under `call_once` (`global_resource.cc:22-29`), and the `concurrency != 0` branch never touches `GlobalResource` \u2014 it only chooses the local-pool path (`segment_helper.cc:809-811`, `index.cc:1440-1446`). No reconfiguration exists in these paths.\n\n## 2. Handoff to the target builder\n\nIn `MixedStreamerReducer::index_build` (`src/core/mixed_reducer/mixed_streamer_reducer.cc:742-777`), one `BorrowedSingleQueueIndexThreads` is constructed over the selected pool (lines 743-744; class at `src/include/zvec/core/framework/index_threads.h:174-225` \u2014 a non-owning adapter whose `count()` is the underlying pool's worker count). That same wrapper is then:\n\n1. passed to the optional conversion-training call `IndexConverter::TrainAndTransform(target_builder_converter_, target_holder, threads)` (lines 745-747), which forwards it as `converter->train(two_pass_holder, std::move(threads))` (`src/core/framework/index_converter.cc:27-36`);\n2. passed to `target_builder_->train(threads, target_holder)` (line 760);\n3. moved into `target_builder_->build(std::move(threads), target_holder)` (line 768).\n\n(The no-threads overloads `train(holder)`/`build(holder)` forward `nullptr` \u2014 `src/include/zvec/core/framework/index_runner.h:687-693, 714-719` \u2014 and are not used on this path.)\n\nPassing vs. consuming: the handoff guarantees the builder *sees* the selected pool, not that every implementation dispatches work onto it. `FlatBuilder::train` ignores it outright (`src/core/algorithm/flat/flat_builder.h:47-51`). DiskANN's `train` uses it only for its worker count (below), while DiskANN's `build` genuinely executes on it: `build_internal`/`prune_internal`/`generate_quantized_data` submit `threads->count()` tasks via `threads->make_group()` (`src/core/algorithm/diskann/diskann_builder.cc:304-315, 341-352, 451-481`, invoked from `build` at 762-777). A null `threads` would trigger a fallback owned pool sized `build_thread_count_` (`diskann_builder.cc:657-663, 714-720`), but on this path `threads` is always non-null.\n\n## 3. DiskANN PQ training: the chunk-dispatch pool is *not* reused\n\n`DiskAnnBuilder::train(threads, holder)` (`diskann_builder.cc:633-681`) calls `train_quantized_data(threads)` (line 665). There (lines 378-427):\n\n- `pq_thread_count = max(1, min(build_thread_count_, threads->count()))` (lines 392-393), with the explicit comment \"The quantizer creates its own pool, so bound it by the caller's pool\" (line 391);\n- it is injected as the `\"thread_count\"` init param (line 394), which `PqInt8Quantizer::init` stores into `thread_count_` (`src/turbo/quantizer/pq_int8_quantizer/pq_int8_quantizer.cc:142`);\n- training then runs via the one-argument `quantizer_->train(training_holder)` (line 406). Note the base `turbo::Quantizer::train(holder, thread_count)` simply discards `thread_count` (`src/turbo/quantizer/quantizer.h:120-122`), so the bound travels exclusively through the init param, not the train call.\n\n`PqInt8Quantizer::train` (`pq_int8_quantizer.cc:232-317`) calls `train_all_chunks` (lines 298 and 305), which **constructs a fresh, quantizer-owned `SingleQueueIndexThreads(thread_count_, false)` per invocation** (lines 319-323; ctor at `index_threads.h:113-116` uses the size as-is when > 0, still ThreadPool-clamped), dispatches one task per worker striding the PQ chunks (lines 328-343), waits (line 356), and lets the pool die with the function. So the caller's borrowed pool is never used to run PQ k-means; it only *bounds* a newly created pool.\n\nWorker count relative to caller and builder setting: `build_thread_count_` comes from `PARAM_DISKANN_BUILDER_THREAD_COUNT` (`diskann_builder.cc:51`; key `\"zvec.diskann.builder.thread_count\"` at `src/core/algorithm/diskann/diskann_params.h:31-32`), and defaults to `hardware_concurrency` when 0/unset (`diskann_builder.cc:53-55`; member default 0 at `diskann_builder.h:111`; no other setter of that param exists in this checkout). Therefore the PQ pool gets `min(build_thread_count_, caller_count)` workers: **4 in scenario A** (min(hw, 4) with hw \u2265 4) and **2 in scenario B** \u2014 freshly created each time, in both cases.\n\n## 4. What the settings do and do not establish\n\n**Established (pool capacity):**\n- Scenario A's merge borrows the global optimize pool of exactly 4 workers (`global_resource.cc:27-29` + `thread_pool.cc:93-101` clamp); scenario B's merge borrows a temporary 2-worker pool that dies with `Index::merge` (`index.cc:1434-1445`).\n- The DiskANN PQ chunk-dispatch pool is a per-call owned pool of `max(1, min(build_thread_count_, caller_pool_count))` workers (`diskann_builder.cc:392-394`, `pq_int8_quantizer.cc:322-323`): 4 under A, 2 under B.\n- All pool sizes are clamped to `[1, hardware_concurrency]` (`thread_pool.cc:85-101`).\n\n**Not established (process threads / CPU use):**\n- Nothing here bounds the *whole process's* thread count. Independent thread resources coexist: the global query pool (`global_resource.cc:24-26`), per-call pools elsewhere (e.g., other quantizers' `train_all_chunks`), any threads the OPQ/k-means internals or DiskANN I/O backend use, plus Python-interpreter and OS threads. These settings describe specific pools, not a process-wide cap.\n- Worker count is capacity, not utilization. Pool workers idle when queues are empty; actual parallelism is further limited by task granularity (PQ submits exactly `pool_count` tasks striding `num_chunk_` chunks, `pq_int8_quantizer.cc:330-343`, so effective parallelism is `min(pool_count, num_chunk_)`), by serialized phases (sample collection, normalization, `serialize`, graph stages), and by I/O. With binding disabled (`thread_pool.cc:93-112`, `config.cc:43`), no CPU pinning is implied either. So one can conclude \"pool X has N workers available to this stage,\" but not \"the process runs N threads\" or \"N cores are busy.\""
  },
